WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:06.700
The general idea of this work is reliable sparse representation learning under uncertainty.

2
00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:15.420
We design a heteroscedastic orthogonal transform learning algorithm for both Gaussian denoising and masked inpainting.

3
00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:22.350
In this photo, the noise strength changes from block to block.

4
00:00:23.273 --> 00:00:26.653
Some patches are barely corrupted; others are buried in noise.

5
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.120
We model each eight-by-eight patch as y equals x plus noise, with a known variance v.

6
00:00:32.751 --> 00:00:35.251
A small v means a reliable observation.

7
00:00:36.363 --> 00:00:39.663
A standard denoiser uses one noise level for every patch.

8
00:00:39.847 --> 00:00:45.827
So it erases real detail where the data are reliable, and leaves noise where they are not.

9
00:00:46.871 --> 00:00:49.531
Our second estimate comes from a sparse model.

10
00:00:49.845 --> 00:00:53.965
A learned orthogonal transform, Q, turns the patch into sixty-four coefficients.

11
00:00:55.255 --> 00:00:59.135
Thresholding keeps only the significant ones: the sparse code, z.

12
00:00:59.986 --> 00:01:04.886
Mapping back gives the prediction, x tilde: clean-looking, but it can miss real texture.

13
00:01:06.937 --> 00:01:11.237
So each patch has two estimates of the same clean image. Which should we trust?

14
00:01:11.257 --> 00:01:13.817
Add one dial, delta, that blends them.

15
00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:20.410
At delta equal to zero we trust the model completely: hard consistency, and texture is lost.

16
00:01:21.302 --> 00:01:25.902
As delta grows, the weight shifts back to the observation, until the noise returns.

17
00:01:27.368 --> 00:01:31.828
The best blend lies in between, and noisier patches lean more on the model.

18
00:01:33.472 --> 00:01:35.472
The dial comes from one objective.

19
00:01:35.818 --> 00:01:38.538
Fit the observation, weighted by its noise variance;

20
00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.770
stay close to the sparse model, with a penalty of one over delta;

21
00:01:43.206 --> 00:01:44.976
and keep the code sparse.

22
00:01:45.346 --> 00:01:49.847
Delta going to zero forces exact agreement: hard consistency.

23
00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:53.667
And solving for the clean patch gives exactly our blend.

24
00:01:54.398 --> 00:01:58.958
Eliminating x is exact: delta simply adds to the noise variance.

25
00:01:59.181 --> 00:02:06.281
So a less trusted model also learns more cautiously: each patch gets less weight, and the coding threshold rises.

26
00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.660
Where should the dial sit? Compare both estimates with the true patch.

27
00:02:11.858 --> 00:02:17.538
The observation errs by the noise, with variance v. The prediction errs by e, with energy r.

28
00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:23.558
If these errors were unrelated, the best blend weights each estimate by its inverse variance.

29
00:02:23.578 --> 00:02:28.798
So delta equals r: distrust the model exactly as much as it errs.

30
00:02:29.114 --> 00:02:34.706
But the prediction was learned from the same noisy patch, so its error partly copies the noise.

31
00:02:34.726 --> 00:02:42.126
The cross moment kappa measures that overlap, and shifts the optimum, whenever kappa is below both r and v.

32
00:02:43.114 --> 00:02:49.592
Both moments need the clean patch, so we calibrate on a clean reference, with many independent noise draws.

33
00:02:49.612 --> 00:02:56.268
On each draw, the inner learner alternates thresholding and a reliability-weighted update of Q.

34
00:02:57.162 --> 00:03:03.910
Averaging the errors gives r and kappa for every patch, and each patch picks its lowest-risk delta.

35
00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:08.640
A new delta changes what is learned, so we relearn and measure again.

36
00:03:09.339 --> 00:03:11.379
Then the delta map is frozen.

37
00:03:11.578 --> 00:03:17.819
For a new noisy observation of the same image, a fresh Q and Z are learned, without the clean reference,

38
00:03:17.839 --> 00:03:21.399
and each patch is blended with its frozen delta.

39
00:03:22.141 --> 00:03:30.301
On the castle, PSNR rises from 25.9 dB with one noise scale, to 27.8 dB with Hetero-Soft.

40
00:03:30.971 --> 00:03:34.671
In this window, edges survive with less residual noise.

41
00:03:35.604 --> 00:03:41.804
Across BSD68, the mean rises from 25.2 to 27.3 dB.

42
00:03:43.206 --> 00:03:45.850
The same idea extends to missing pixels.

43
00:03:46.237 --> 00:03:53.072
The fraction of observed pixels, rho, gives a reliability proxy: one minus rho, over rho.

44
00:03:53.092 --> 00:04:00.467
Observed pixels stay fixed, and sparse predictions fill the gaps, with one shared delta selected on Set12.

45
00:04:00.487 --> 00:04:06.517
Here the missing-pixel detail improves from 12.6 to 17.2 dB.

46
00:04:07.543 --> 00:04:11.203
Observation reliability and model reliability set one rule:

47
00:04:11.817 --> 00:04:18.061
how much each patch teaches the transform, which coefficients survive, and how the estimate is blended.

48
00:04:18.711 --> 00:04:22.551
Use local reliability throughout learning and reconstruction.
